/* ============================================================
   ANSWER KEY - Introduction to SQL & Databases (MEDIUM LEVEL)
   Curriculum:   RetailMart V3
   Note: Day 1 is concept-only, so these are short reference
   answers (no queries to run/verify) matching each question.
   ============================================================ */

/* Q1.  SQL wins for 100GB: it processes data on-disk/streamed without needing full RAM and uses an optimizer for joins/aggregates. pandas wins for iterative, custom transformations once data is filtered down to something that fits memory, and for tight Python-native ML integration. */
/* Q2.  SQL on a database refreshes live from the source of truth with indexed, fast queries; Python loading CSVs re-reads and re-parses static files each time, is slower, and drifts out of sync with live data. */
/* Q3.  When relationships matter more than tabular aggregates - e.g. RetailMart fraud rings linking shared cards/addresses/devices across many customers - a graph DB traverses those links far more naturally than repeated SQL self-joins. */
/* Q4.  (1) LLMs generate SQL but a human must still verify correctness against the real schema; (2) SQL remains the interface between AI tools and every production database, so prompting AI well requires understanding SQL; (3) debugging/optimizing AI-generated queries at scale still requires real SQL skill. */
/* Q5.  MongoDB+AI can store/generate free-text answers but can't guarantee ACID transactions, can't easily do multi-collection joins, and gives no built-in way to enforce referential integrity - all things a real RDBMS is built for. */
/* Q6.  Excel emails have no audit trail of who changed what, no way to guarantee correctness across an org-wide shared file, and no ability to reliably query millions of transactional rows - a shared database with proper access control eliminates all three risks. */
/* Q7.  Both use SELECT/JOIN/GROUP BY daily; Analysts write more read-only exploratory/reporting SQL, while Engineers write more DDL, ETL pipeline SQL, and performance-tuned production queries that other systems depend on. */
/* Q8.  Example: sales dropped 15% last week, so instead of manually filtering an Excel export, I wrote a single grouped SQL query joining orders/returns/dates that instantly isolated the exact store and date range causing it - a Python script would have taken far longer to write and rerun. */
/* Q9.  Roughly a dozen+: validate stock (SELECT), lock/decrement inventory (UPDATE), insert the order + order_items (INSERT), record payment (INSERT), log the event (INSERT), because checkout touches inventory, orders, payments, and audit tables all in one flow. */
/* Q10. DBT solves the "SQL scripts run in the right order, are tested, and are version-controlled" problem - it turns ad-hoc SQL into a maintainable, testable transformation pipeline with dependency management. */
/* Q11. Because each database has a different query planner/optimizer with different cost models, statistics, and available access paths, so the same logical query can be executed via very different physical strategies. */
/* Q12. Scale: SQL handles millions of rows, Excel chokes; Correctness: SQL enforces types/constraints, Excel allows silent errors; Repeatability: SQL queries rerun identically, Excel steps are manual and drift; Audit: SQL has query logs/history, Excel has none. */
/* Q13. (1) You must judge whether AI-generated SQL is actually correct; (2) most interview problems test reasoning about data, which AI can't do for you live; (3) production systems still need humans who can read/debug/optimize real queries. */
/* Q14. Pulling and joining training data from relational sources, validating data quality with SQL before modeling, and writing SQL to evaluate model outputs against ground-truth tables stored in a database. */
/* Q15. Banking/fintech (analyst), e-commerce (data analyst), healthcare (health data analyst), telecom (BI analyst), and consulting/audit (data analyst) - all pay a premium for strong SQL. */
/* Q16. Data: "150000" in sales.orders; Information: "RetailMart processed 150,000 orders this quarter, up 12% YoY" - the same raw row count turned into a meaningful business statement. */
/* Q17. Connect to the warehouse, write a SELECT joining sales.orders and core.dim_date filtered to yesterday, aggregate the total, sanity-check against yesterday's expectations, then share the number/screenshot with the stakeholder. */
/* Q18. SQL queries are reusable and consistent for every analyst on the team; Excel workflows are manual, click-based, and hard to standardize as headcount grows, so results diverge between people doing "the same" analysis. */
/* Q19. Declarative SQL: SELECT customer_id FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id ORDER BY SUM(amount) DESC LIMIT 10 - states the desired result. Imperative Python: loop over every order, accumulate totals in a dict, sort the dict, and slice the top 10 - states each step. */
/* Q20. Losing type safety (a "date" stored as text can't be range-filtered efficiently), losing built-in validation (typos/garbage pass silently), and losing query performance (no numeric/date indexes possible on raw text). */
/* Q21. Click happens in the app -> INSERT into sales.orders/order_items (database) -> nightly/streaming ETL job (SQL) -> analyst's dashboard queries the warehouse (SQL) -> chart rendered for the business user. */
/* Q22. A "data swamp" is a data store that's grown disorganized and untrustworthy - duplicated, undocumented, low-quality data nobody can rely on; disciplined SQL practice (constraints, naming, documented schemas) keeps data clean and queryable instead. */
/* Q23. MongoDB's flexible schema becomes a liability once the business needs consistent reporting, joins across entities, and transactional guarantees - PostgreSQL provides all three natively, so teams migrate as they mature. */
/* Q24. OLTP handles fast, high-volume day-to-day transactions (placing an order); OLAP is optimized for complex analytical queries over historical data (quarterly revenue trends). In RetailMart, sales.orders inserts are OLTP-style; a revenue rollup across finance.revenue_summary is OLAP-style. */
/* Q25. SQL - nearly every analyst role touches a database daily and most Python analysis still starts by pulling data via SQL, so a deep SQL engineer unlocks more of the team's day-to-day work than a Python-only specialist would. */

/* Q26. PostgreSQL, for strict accounting needs - it has stronger built-in constraint/type support (NUMERIC, CHECK constraints) and full ACID transactions that fintech ledgers depend on, without extra configuration MySQL sometimes needs. */
/* Q27. SQLite fits a mobile/desktop app with a single local user and no concurrent writers, or a lightweight prototype/testing setup - not a multi-user server workload. */
/* Q28. Snowflake's columnar storage and elastic compute scale to terabytes/petabytes with near-zero tuning, while PostgreSQL (row-store) needs manual indexing/partitioning and a fixed server to approach that scale. */
/* Q29. OLTP optimizes for fast single-row reads/writes (row-oriented storage, heavy indexing on keys); OLAP optimizes for scanning huge column ranges for aggregation (columnar storage, minimal per-row overhead). */
/* Q30. BigQuery handles massive ad-hoc analytical queries cheaply at scale; PostgreSQL handles the low-latency transactional workload the live app needs - each is purpose-built for a different access pattern. */
/* Q31. Losing Atomicity risks half-completed transfers; losing Consistency risks broken constraints/balances; losing Isolation risks concurrent transactions reading each other's uncommitted changes; losing Durability risks losing committed money after a crash. */
/* Q32. A primary with read replicas wins for read-heavy, moderate-write workloads needing simplicity; a shared-nothing distributed DB wins when write throughput/data volume exceeds what a single primary can handle. */
/* Q33. Redis is an in-memory cache/store that's typically not durable by default and has no relational query/join capability - it's meant to sit alongside PostgreSQL as a speed layer, not replace its transactional, queryable source of truth. */
/* Q34. Eventual consistency means replicas may briefly show stale data after a write; analyst tools computing exact revenue/inventory numbers can't tolerate that lag without risking visibly wrong reports. */
/* Q35. Permissions would be harder to scope per team, table names could collide, the schema browser would become unmanageable, and query performance/planning intuition (knowing where things live) would get much harder. */
/* Q36. PostgreSQL has no per-core licensing cost, a large open-source extension ecosystem, and strong community/cloud support - all of which fit a startup avoiding vendor lock-in and licensing bills. */
/* Q37. Row-oriented storage reads whole rows even if you only need "category + revenue," wasting I/O; columnar storage reads only those two columns across all rows, making a "monthly revenue per category" scan far faster. */
/* Q38. NULL is defined as "unknown," so PostgreSQL treats every NULL as distinct from every other NULL (not equal to each other), allowing multiple NULLs in a UNIQUE column; a PRIMARY KEY additionally forbids NULL entirely by definition, so only one "identity" per row is allowed. */
/* Q39. You pay for enterprise support, specialized features (advanced partitioning, built-in HA tooling), certifications, and vendor accountability - PostgreSQL gives you the core engine free but you self-support or pay a third party. */
/* Q40. RDS: less operational overhead (managed patching/backups) but less low-level control and a recurring premium; self-hosted EC2: full control and often cheaper at scale but you own patching, backups, and failover; RDS is usually the safer default for smaller teams. */
/* Q41. Relational databases enforce cross-row consistency (foreign keys, joins, transactions) that becomes very hard to maintain once data is split across many machines, whereas NoSQL databases were designed from the start without those cross-node guarantees. */
/* Q42. A UPI transfer either fully completes - money leaves your account and arrives in the merchant's - or it fully fails and nothing moves; a database transaction is that same "all or nothing" guarantee applied to a group of SQL statements. */
/* Q43. Both startups eventually need consistent multi-entity reporting (finance, ops) and enforce data integrity across growing feature sets - a document store that seemed schema-flexible early on turns into a bolt-on relational layer later. */
/* Q44. Running heavy analytical scans on the OLTP primary competes for the same resources live customer transactions need, risking slow checkouts; a replica absorbs analytics load without affecting production writes. */
/* Q45. A view is a saved, named SELECT statement you can query like a table; it's useful because it centralizes and reuses complex logic instead of every analyst pasting/maintaining the same long query. */
/* Q46. MVCC (Multi-Version Concurrency Control) lets PostgreSQL keep multiple versions of a row so readers never block writers and writers never block readers - each transaction sees a consistent snapshot of the data as of when it started. */
/* Q47. Logical replication streams row-level changes (flexible, works across versions/subsets of tables) and is used for selective sync or upgrades; physical replication copies the exact binary WAL stream and is used for full-database, low-latency HA standbys. */
/* Q48. Each new database connection is expensive to open, and Postgres has a hard per-connection memory cost, so a pool reuses a small set of real connections across many app requests instead of exhausting the database with thousands of direct connections. */
/* Q49. They eventually need consistent transactions, joins across growing feature sets, and reliable reporting that a flexible schema made informally inconsistent - so they end up retrofitting schema validation anyway, the hard way. */
/* Q50. Migrations create a versioned, reviewable, repeatable history of every schema change, so the team (and CI/CD) always knows exactly what state the schema should be in, even on a small database. */

/* Q51. TRUNCATE TABLE is more dangerous day-to-day since it's easy to run accidentally and instantly wipes all data (though structure survives); DROP TABLE is more catastrophic overall since it destroys the table definition too, but both are typically un-recoverable without a backup once committed. */
/* Q52. A code review step, a required WHERE-clause linter/guard, running in a transaction first to eyeball the affected row count, and least-privilege access (no direct prod write access) could each have stopped it. */
/* Q53. Early PostgreSQL traded flexibility for simplicity/safety: locking the whole table during ALTER TABLE avoided complex partial-visibility bugs, at the cost of blocking concurrent reads/writes during the change (later versions relaxed this for many ALTER types). */
/* Q54. Schema-level GRANT/REVOKE lets you manage access for a whole business domain (e.g. all of "hr") in one statement, instead of repeating the same grant on dozens of individual tables and having to remember every new table added later. */
/* Q55. COMMIT permanently writes the new row's data to disk/WAL and makes it visible to other transactions; ROLLBACK discards the in-progress row version entirely, as if the INSERT never happened - nothing is written to the visible table state. */
/* Q56. An accidental TRUNCATE instantly and silently wipes an entire table with no undo, so teams ban it in production scripts to force a slower, deliberate, reviewed DELETE (or a manual/approved truncate) instead. */
/* Q57. ANSI types (INT, VARCHAR, DATE) are portable across databases, making migration easier; PostgreSQL extensions (JSONB, ARRAY, UUID) are powerful and often faster for their use case, but lock you into PostgreSQL-specific behavior. */
/* Q58. TRUNCATE deallocates entire data pages directly rather than deleting row-by-row, so it never fires the per-row triggers that DELETE, which does process (and can cascade logic on) each row individually, does trigger. */
/* Q59. Missing/unused index on a target column, table/row locks from concurrent transactions, disk I/O saturation, an oversized batch in one statement, or AUTOVACUUM/checkpoint activity competing for resources at that moment. */
/* Q60. Auto-commit surprises juniors when a single bad statement (like an unintended UPDATE) commits instantly with no chance to inspect/rollback; explicit BEGIN/COMMIT surprises them when they forget to COMMIT and think their change "didn't work." */
/* Q61. ON CONFLICT lets the database handle the insert-or-update decision atomically in one statement, avoiding the race condition where two concurrent try/catch/update flows in app code could both "insert" and violate a unique constraint. */
/* Q62. Every row in the 5-million-row table gets updated, not just the intended one - a huge, slow, likely-locking operation that silently corrupts data across the whole table until someone notices and restores from backup. */
/* Q63. INSERT ... SELECT wins for data already inside the database (fast, no network/parsing overhead); COPY FROM file wins for bulk-loading external files since it streams and parses far faster than row-by-row INSERT statements. */
/* Q64. It grants every privilege to every user/role on the database, removing any meaningful access control and making it trivial for a compromised or careless account to read/modify/drop anything. */
/* Q65. SAVEPOINT marks a point inside a transaction you can roll back to without discarding the whole transaction - useful for retrying one risky statement (e.g. an insert that might violate a constraint) while keeping earlier work intact. */
/* Q66. Explicit transactions are safer for analysts running ad-hoc queries, since auto-commit mode would instantly commit an accidental UPDATE/DELETE with no chance to review before it's permanent. */
/* Q67. As a company adds more schemas and more tables share similar names across them, an unqualified name becomes ambiguous or wrong - the schema prefix removes any doubt about exactly which table is meant. */
/* Q68. (1) Sorting/filtering by "date" as text is unreliable and can sort lexicographically instead of chronologically; (2) date arithmetic (add 30 days) isn't possible on text; (3) invalid "dates" like "13/45/2024" pass silently with no validation. */
/* Q69. Quoted identifiers preserve exact case/special characters for flexibility, but most teams ban them because every future reference must also be quoted exactly right, making typos easy and queries error-prone for no real benefit. */
/* Q70. Common causes: someone changed the schema (dropped/renamed a column), a permissions change, data grew and a timeout was hit, a dependent view/function changed, or stale statistics led the planner to a bad new plan. */
/* Q71. INSERT INTO ... SELECT wins when data already lives in the database, since Postgres builds the new table in one internal operation without re-parsing external text; CREATE TABLE then INSERT wins when you need the structure defined first (constraints, defaults) before data arrives incrementally. */
/* Q72. NUMERIC stores exact decimal values with no rounding error, which matters for money where FLOAT's binary approximation can silently produce off-by-a-cent errors that compound at scale. */
/* Q73. Column order doesn't affect query correctness/results, but it can affect on-disk storage size slightly due to type-alignment padding, and it affects the default order columns appear in SELECT *. */
/* Q74. CHECK constraints belong in the database when the rule must hold no matter which application/script touches the data; application-side validation is better for rules that change often or depend on business logic outside plain data shape. */
/* Q75. Almost every production table benefits from knowing when a row was created/last modified, for auditing, debugging, incremental ETL loads, and answering "when did this change" questions later. */

/* Q76. Splitting orders (one row per order, header info) from order_items (one row per line item) avoids repeating order-level data on every item and correctly models the real one-to-many relationship between an order and its products. */
/* Q77. Brand and category each change far less often than a product's own attributes, and many products can share one brand/category - keeping them in separate dimension tables avoids duplicating brand/category text on every product row and keeps updates centralized. */
/* Q78. Employee identity/role data changes rarely, while pay_slip records accumulate every pay period - splitting them avoids bloating the core employee table with a growing history and lets payroll be secured/audited separately from HR records. */
/* Q79. page_views is extremely high-volume, append-only clickstream data with a very different access pattern (write-heavy, rarely joined transactionally) than customer or sales data, so isolating it in web_events keeps those other schemas lean and keeps permissions/retention policies separate. */
/* Q80. sales.payments likely records the transactional payment tied directly to an order at checkout time, while finance.payments likely reflects the finance team's reconciled/accounting view of that same money - separating operational and financial-ledger perspectives. */
/* Q81. customers.tier is likely a quick denormalized snapshot for fast lookups, while loyalty.tiers/members holds the full rules and history - the redundancy trades a little duplication for query speed on the hot customer-lookup path. */
/* Q82. A customer can have multiple addresses (billing, shipping, multiple homes); is_default flags which one to use automatically when no address is explicitly chosen, without needing a separate single-address column. */
/* Q83. Keeping check_in/check_out as separate timestamps preserves the raw source-of-truth data and lets you compute duration, late arrivals, or early departures flexibly later, rather than baking one fixed calculation into storage. */
/* Q84. A pre-populated date dimension lets you join for calendar attributes (day name, fiscal quarter, holiday flags) with zero computation per query, and lets business calendars (fiscal year, holidays) be defined once rather than recalculated with EXTRACT() every time. */
/* Q85. A free-text 'level' column (INFO/WARN/ERROR) is simple, human-readable, and flexible for logging without needing a lookup join just to interpret a severity - the small loss of referential integrity is worth the simplicity for a high-volume log table. */
/* Q86. Storing gross_total directly avoids recomputing a SUM over order_items on every order lookup (fast reads), while order_items remains the detailed source of truth for validation, recalculation, or returns - a deliberate normalize/denormalize tradeoff. */
/* Q87. TEXT keeps transcripts simple to store and full-text search on with standard SQL, at the cost of losing JSONB's structured querying (e.g. filtering by speaker or sentiment fields) unless that structure is added later. */
/* Q88. sales.shipments likely tracks outbound shipments to customers, while supply_chain.shipments tracks inbound shipments from suppliers to warehouses - same word, different business process, so the schema separation is essential to avoid conflating the two. */
/* Q89. Tables where all times are assumed to occur in one local business timezone (in-store events) can safely use plain TIMESTAMP, while tables that may span timezones (web/API events from multiple regions) need TIMESTAMPTZ to store an unambiguous instant in time. */
/* Q90. dim_* tables are shared, foundational reference data used across many schemas (a region or date applies to sales, hr, and stores alike), so they live in a neutral 'core' schema rather than being owned by any one business domain. */
/* Q91. The composite key (warehouse_id, product_id, snapshot_date) reflects that inventory is naturally unique per warehouse, per product, per point in time - no single column alone identifies a snapshot row. */
/* Q92. Category likely belongs to the brand, not the product directly (via dim_brand -> dim_category), so adding a redundant category column on products would duplicate data that's already derivable through the brand relationship. */
/* Q93. Every customer places roughly 3 orders on average in this dataset, which is a realistic ratio for a retail business - most customers buy more than once but not dramatically more, matching real-world purchase frequency. */
/* Q94. Loyalty membership is inherently one-to-one with a customer (a customer either is or isn't a loyalty member), so reusing customer_id as the primary key avoids an unnecessary extra surrogate key and makes the relationship explicit. */
/* Q95. No - dept_name belongs in core.dim_department and should stay joined via dept_id, since duplicating it as a VARCHAR on every employee row risks inconsistent department names if the department is ever renamed. */
/* Q96. Because access is granted per-schema (GRANT ... ON SCHEMA hr TO hr_role), the schema boundary itself acts as the permission boundary, so a single unified permissions table isn't needed - PostgreSQL's own GRANT system on schemas/tables does that job. */
/* Q97. request_id is likely a UUID or externally-generated trace identifier (possibly from a load balancer or API gateway) rather than a simple sequential integer, so TEXT accommodates any ID format without assuming a numeric sequence. */
/* Q98. The inconsistency (cust_id vs customer_id) is a real design flaw - it likely arose from different tables being modeled by different people/times; a well-maintained schema should standardize on one naming convention for the same concept. */
/* Q99. It's a small, mostly-static lookup table - all it needs to represent is which id maps to which category name, so two columns are sufficient without any wasted structure. */
/* Q100. RetailMart's 16 schemas and what each covers, the difference between fact and dimension tables, how DDL/DML/DCL/TCL differ, the sql_practice-vs-isct_retailmart safety rule, and how to read a schema-qualified table name. */

/* ============================================================
   END OF ANSWER KEY - MEDIUM LEVEL (100/100)
============================================================ */
